("32.2","chapter-32-cal.md","FACT",[("She was bitten on a job when she was twenty-six. She told me so on the first night, a minute after I told her his name. In three months he had leaned on her shin at lunch and lain against her leg on a beach. At Carmel she had put a hand on his back once, on the log. She had not done it since."+P,""),("She stood in the doorway with her hands in the sleeves of my fleece.","She had her hands in the sleeves of my fleece.")],
 "Wrong: in 27, with Cal there holding the dog's head, she towels Pancake down on the asphalt, feeds him a treat and wipes his face (“I'd never had my hands on a dog that long in my life”). Without the paragraph, her stopping in the doorway and then coming in carries it. The second change keeps two doorway sentences from landing back to back."),
